          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had a dry cough for about ten days after a cold, no fever. Should I be doing anything?",
          "answer": "**The emergency result above was triggered by my own search wording, not by anything you described.** I included \"chest pain\" in the question I sent to the risk tool, and the tool matched it. You have not told me about chest pain. Still, if you have chest pain, pressure or tightness, or sudden breathlessness, call emergency services now. Do not drive yourself.\n\n**About your cough:** The retrieved guidance on the common cold says cough often lasts two to three weeks after the other symptoms have gone, and that this is normal. It is not by itself a sign of bacterial infection or treatment failure (common cold guidance). The influenza guidance also notes that a worsening cough after several days of improvement can be a sign of a secondary lung infection (pneumonia), and that this pattern is worth recognising (influenza guidance). So the key question is whether your cough is getting better or worse over time.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- **Breathlessness:** The anaphylaxis guidance lists breathlessness and chest tightness as signs that need emergency care when they come with other allergic symptoms. The retrieved passages do not give a standalone rule for breathlessness after a cold, so please ask a clinician about it promptly.\n- **Chest pain:** Chest pain can signal a heart problem and needs emergency assessment (chest pain guidance).\n- **Coughing up blood, high fever, or symptoms getting worse:** My retrieved passages do not cover coughing up blood or high fever as red flags. Please do not treat that gap as reassurance. Get medical advice promptly if you notice either one.\n\n**What to do:** If your cough is not clearly improving, or it is getting worse, see a clinician soon. The retrieved guidance does not set a precise timeline, so a clinician is the right person to decide how urgent your case is.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
